WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.901
It is the lunch rush.

00:00:05.901 --> 00:00:09.802
A food delivery app gets an order, and needs every driver within two kilometers.

00:00:09.802 --> 00:00:13.703
The dumb way measures the distance to all ten million driver locations.

00:00:13.703 --> 00:00:17.604
A full table scan, ten million reads, on every order.

00:00:17.604 --> 00:00:21.505
At peak, that one query melts the database.

00:00:21.505 --> 00:00:26.130
The fix is to group by location.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:30.756
Chop the map into a grid, code each cell, store orders by it.

00:00:30.756 --> 00:00:35.381
Nearby orders share a code, so find nearby becomes find the same code.

00:00:35.381 --> 00:00:40.007
The obvious shape is a square, the classic geo hash.

00:00:40.007 --> 00:00:44.632
It groups points fine, but a radius query reads the cell plus its ring of neighbors, and a square's ring is uneven.

00:00:44.632 --> 00:00:47.986
Here is the problem with squares.

00:00:47.986 --> 00:00:51.340
A square has eight neighbors, but at two distances.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.694
The four edges are one cell away.

00:00:54.694 --> 00:00:58.048
The four corners are farther, by a factor of root two.

00:00:58.048 --> 00:01:01.402
So the ring you read is lopsided.

00:01:01.402 --> 00:01:04.756
A hexagon fixes it.

00:01:04.756 --> 00:01:08.110
Its six neighbors are all exactly the same distance away.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:11.464
One distance, an even ring in every direction.

00:01:11.464 --> 00:01:14.818
That is why Uber and Google grid the world in hexagons, a system called H three.

00:01:14.818 --> 00:01:18.719
First, the four places on this map.

00:01:18.719 --> 00:01:22.620
SF Mission, up north in the city.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:26.521
And three Stanford buildings, NVidia, Packard, and Gates, clustered tightly together to the south.

00:01:26.521 --> 00:01:30.422
Three close, one far.

00:01:30.422 --> 00:01:35.633
Now their actual H3 codes.

00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:40.843
At resolution eight, zoomed out, the three campus buildings get the identical code, one shared cell.

00:01:40.843 --> 00:01:46.054
SF Mission, twenty-nine miles away, gets a different one.

00:01:46.054 --> 00:01:51.264
Zoom in to resolution twelve, and the three campus codes split into separate cells, differing at the end, while SF stays different.

00:01:51.264 --> 00:01:56.475
Nearby points share a cell, far ones never do.

00:01:56.475 --> 00:02:00.020
So here is the scheme.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.565
Step one, the hash.

00:02:03.565 --> 00:02:07.109
Start with the orders, ten million of them, each just a latitude and longitude.

00:02:07.109 --> 00:02:10.654
Then lay H3's hexagons over the map.

00:02:10.654 --> 00:02:14.199
Now every order falls in one hexagon, and takes its hexagon's code.

00:02:14.199 --> 00:02:17.744
Orders nearby land in the same hexagon, so they share the same code.

00:02:17.744 --> 00:02:21.500
Step two, the lookup.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:25.256
A driver asks for orders within two kilometers.

00:02:25.256 --> 00:02:29.012
Hash the location, and you get one cell, one lookup.

00:02:29.012 --> 00:02:32.768
But the radius crosses cell borders, so you also read the six neighbors around it.

00:02:32.768 --> 00:02:36.523
That is seven cells, on up to seven different pages, so up to seven reads.

00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:40.279
Here is the part to get right.

00:02:40.279 --> 00:02:44.035
It is not one read.

00:02:44.035 --> 00:02:47.791
It is seven, and seven is constant, whether there are ten thousand orders or ten million.

00:02:47.791 --> 00:02:53.522
Geo-hashing turns a two-dimensional coordinate into a one-dimensional key, so find within two kilometers becomes a handful of cell lookups, not distance math over millions of rows.

00:02:53.522 --> 00:02:59.252
That count stays around seven, whatever the order count, and hexagons keep the ring even.

00:02:59.252 --> 00:03:04.983
It is an exact spatial hash.

00:03:04.983 --> 00:03:10.713
Next we bend the idea: a hash that makes similar things collide on purpose, to search by similarity.

00:03:10.713 --> 00:03:16.444
That is LSH.
